# Tracker reporting

Two write cases: a **QA result comment** on an existing ticket, and a **new
bug**. Both are prepared by `tracker-reporter`, shown to the user, and posted
only after explicit approval. The MCP hook enforces the gate; this rule
defines the content.

## QA result comment — one per test run

```
QA — Pass | Fail | Partial | In progress

Tested on: <environment> | <date>
Build: <version / branch / commit>

Covered:
- <exact scenario> → pass | fail | partial

Issues found:
- <one line, reproducible> → <BUG-ID or "logged">

Notes:
- <context the developer or next tester needs>
```

Name exact scenarios, never "happy path tested". Always state environment and
build. No Pass without execution evidence in the ticket file. If the user
declines to post, leave the comment in `tickets/{ID}.md` for manual paste.
Never claim it was posted unless the post succeeded.

## New bug — short and reproducible

Title: `<Surface> | <what is broken, one line>` (surfaces: `BE`, `Web`,
`iOS`, `Android`, or the project's own list).

Body, exactly these sections:

```
Description       1–3 sentences: what is broken (INTERMITTENT + frequency if so)
Steps to reproduce  numbered
Expected
Actual
Technical evidence  optional and short: status code, key log line, file:symbol
```

Fields outside the body: type `Bug`; **Component**, **Priority** and
**Environment** always set (priority guidance: crash / data loss / blocked
main flow = High; workaround exists = Medium; cosmetic or edge = Low); labels
empty unless asked; link the ticket that led to the finding.

Anti-patterns: root-cause essays and suggested fixes in the bug body (they
belong in `tickets/{ID}.md`); environment only in prose; unset priority;
filing without approval.
